 ## Subagents
 
-**Use `subagent_type: "n-agent"` for any subagent you spawn inside a playbook step** (code-writing delegates, ad-hoc helpers). Pass the plugin namespace, as `subagent_type: "nstack:n-agent"`, and set the Agent `effort` per **Match the model to the job**. Explore, search, verify, and mechanical edits already specified step by step run at `medium`, and verify runs on the `swarm workers` model. Implementation runs at `high` on Sonnet and at `medium` on Opus. Per-task diff review runs on the `judgment and prose` model at `high`. An independent verdict that gates a landing, such as a Shipping verifier's, runs on the `swarm workers` model at `high`. Plan writing, hard debugging, design decisions, and the final branch review run on the `judgment and prose` model at `xhigh`. When the branch touches security, auth, billing, persisted data, migrations, concurrency, or a subtle algorithm, the final branch review runs on the `hardest tasks` model instead. These tiers hold at the `large` budget. Another budget moves every tier by the steps it moves the role lines. With no `effort`, the agent runs at the session's effort. The chat that receives `/n-mode` runs the playbook. Never spawn a `n-agent` to run the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`how`, `why`, `interrogate`, `reflect`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type` for diverse-model review. Respect what the skill prescribes, don't override to `n-agent`.
-
-**Defaults for every `Task` call.** Background (the default, so leave `run_in_background` unset), a type that keeps the session's MCP servers (`general-purpose` or `nstack:n-agent`), file pointers not inlined context, explicit model per role (configurable via `/setup-nstack`. Defaults `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast` for code, `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` for prose and judgment). Code delegates tier by difficulty. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) go to your strongest judgment model (`fable xhigh`), whether the task needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence of steps to execute to the letter. Trivial mechanical edits go to your fast code model. Per-role lines in the `/setup-nstack` rule override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). A role with no line keeps its default, and a role line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs that role on the parent chat model (omit Task `model`). Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.
-
-**Match the model to the job.** A role value `<model> <effort>`, such as `opus xhigh`, means Task `model: "opus"` and `effort: "xhigh"`. A value with no effort, such as `haiku`, omits `effort`. Before each spawn, take the model from the role's line or default and the effort from the tiers above. A role line whose effort differs from what the rule's budget gives its default was set by hand, and it wins for that role's own job. A rule whose `# budget` line names one effort, as in `large (xhigh)`, predates budgets that move efforts, so none of its role lines counts as set by hand. Raise the effort one step, up to max, for the hardest or subtlest work in a tier. Change only the effort, and keep the model the role names, so a diverse panel stays diverse. A role on `auto` or `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model. If the Task tool rejects the adjusted effort, do not try another. Use the role's line or default and say so.
+**Use `subagent_type: "n-agent"` for any subagent you spawn inside a playbook step** (code-writing delegates, ad-hoc helpers). Pass the plugin namespace, as `subagent_type: "nstack:n-agent"`, and set its `model` and `effort` from the table in **Match the model to the job**. With no `effort`, the agent runs at the session's effort. The chat that receives `/n-mode` runs the playbook. Never spawn a `n-agent` to run the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`how`, `why`, `interrogate`, `reflect`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type` for diverse-model review. Respect what the skill prescribes, don't override to `n-agent`.
+
+**Defaults for every `Task` call.** Background (the default, so leave `run_in_background` unset), a type that keeps the session's MCP servers (`general-purpose` or `nstack:n-agent`), file pointers not inlined context, explicit model per role (configurable via `/setup-nstack`. Defaults `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast` for code, `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` for prose and judgment). Code delegates tier by difficulty, per the implementation rows of the table in **Match the model to the job**. Those rows name the only models that implement, so never pick another one between them. Pick the tier from the change, not to save cost, and name it in the brief. Per-role lines in the `/setup-nstack` rule override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). A role with no line keeps its default, and a role line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs that role on the parent chat model (omit Task `model`). Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), hard changes read `hard tasks`, and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.
+
+**Match the model to the job.** A role value `<model> <effort>`, such as `opus xhigh`, means Task `model: "opus"` and `effort: "xhigh"`. A value with no effort, such as `haiku`, omits `effort`. Before each spawn, take the model and effort from the job's row:
+
+| Job | Role | Model and effort |
+|---|---|---|
+| Mechanical edit already specified step by step | the code playbook's line | `sonnet medium` |
+| Implementation | the code playbook's line | `sonnet high` |
+| Hard implementation: a new background worker, a change wired through several layers, a migration of the user's saved config or data | `hard tasks` | `opus medium` |
+| Hardest implementation: cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, a subtle algorithm, whether the intent is vague or the steps are exact | `hardest tasks` | `fable xhigh` |
+| Explore and search | `swarm workers` | `sonnet medium` |
+| Verify | `swarm workers` | `sonnet high` |
+| Independent verdict that gates a landing, such as a Shipping verifier's | `swarm workers` | `sonnet high` |
+| Per-task diff review | `judgment and prose` | `opus high` |
+| Plan writing, hard debugging, design decisions | `judgment and prose` | `opus xhigh` |
+| Final branch review | `judgment and prose` | `opus xhigh` |
+| Final branch review when the branch touches security, auth, billing, persisted data, migrations, concurrency, or a subtle algorithm | `hardest tasks` | `fable high` |
+| Panel runners and reviewers (arena, architect, interrogate) | the panel's line | `opus xhigh` and `fable high` |
+
+Fable costs the most, so only the hardest implementation runs it at `xhigh`. These pairs hold at the `large` budget. Another budget moves every effort in the table by the steps it moves the role lines. A role line replaces the model in every row of its role. A role line whose effort differs from what the rule's budget gives its default was set by hand, and its effort replaces the effort in those rows too. A rule whose `# budget` line names one effort, as in `large (xhigh)`, predates budgets that move efforts, so none of its role lines counts as set by hand. Never raise or lower an effort the table and the role lines give. A role on `auto` or `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model. If the Task tool rejects the effort, do not try another. Use the role's line or default and say so.
 
 **Label every spawn.** The CLI line shows only the type and the `description`, and a `general-purpose` type reads `Agent`. So open the description with the model and effort, then two or three words for the task, as in `opus xhigh: review diff`. Write the `model` you pass, or `inherit` when you omit it. Write the `effort` you pass, else the session's. Spawns that routed skills make carry the label too.
 
